    "message": "feat(request-log): store the prompt in its own shared blob\n\nContent addressing landed in 4.47.0 keyed on the whole body, which\ndeduplicated 1.4% of a real log. The reason is structural: a retry or a\nparallel subagent re-sends the same context and gets a different answer\nback, so bodies almost never match even when prompts do.\n\nMeasured across the whole corpus rather than a sample: the prompt is 98%\nof the stored bytes, 29.7% of requests repeat one already stored, and\n35.3% of prompt bytes are exact duplicates. So the prompt now lives in\nits own content-addressed blob, apart from the reply, the reasoning and\nthe tool calls.\n\nOn a copy of the real log, already compressed by 4.47.0: 299 MB -> 209 MB,\n29.9% smaller, 35,461 distinct prompts across 50,460 requests. All 50,460\nbodies verified identical afterwards. Cumulative from the original\nuncompressed database, 1.73 GB -> 209 MB.\n\nBoth layouts coexist without a version flag: a blob written before the\nsplit still carries its prompt inside it, and the absence of that key is\nwhat says a blob holds only the reply. Reads merge the two, and search\nconsiders them together, because \"proxy 8082\" can have one word in the\nprompt and the other in the reasoning.\n\nCompaction splits existing combined blobs in a second pass, and keeps the\nwrite path's compression level. Turning it up looked free for a one-time\npass, but level 19 measured only 4.9% smaller than level 9 (10.57x\nagainst 10.08x) at a ninth of the speed -- three hours instead of twenty\nminutes on a full log.\n\nAlso relaxes the NOT NULL on the reply reference, which a request with a\nprompt and no reply now legitimately leaves empty.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
